people's perceptions have a way of borrowing deep into our minds. Is it because I'm too tall? Do they think I'm fat? Do I even belong here? This week on Hidden Brain, how our worries about the stereotypes people hold of us can shape everything from parent-teacher conferences to police shootings. We all have memories of summers gone by, of long walks and lazy afternoons, relaxing by a pool, vacations with friends and family. Claude Steele has a less pleasant memory of one long ago summer. He was with a group of kids coming home from school. It was in 1950s and they were in Phoenix, a small town just out of Chicago. You know, we were just walking along each of us peeling off as we got closer to our houses.

